+## Asking for the list over the socket
+
+- Serving a list is its own switch, `transport.history`, and not part of `transport.resume`: resume on with history off still refuses the frame. Rejoining hands a client back a conversation it was already in, while a list tells it what else the user has, and a deployer who agreed to the first did not thereby agree to the second ([validated by: refuses the frame with resume on but history off, since one switch is not the other](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L250)).
+- With `history.enabled`, a `list_conversations` frame is answered with exactly one `conversation_list` and nothing else — no frame belonging to a turn, no `stream_end` — carrying the user's conversations newest activity first, each `createdAt` and `updatedAt` an ISO string rather than a `Date`, which is what survives the wire ([validated by: answers the user's conversations newest first, with both times as ISO strings](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L99)).
+- The frame is built from four fields and nothing else, so a store answering whole sessions — entries, credentials, another user's id among them — still puts only a summary on the wire ([validated by: sends only the four summary fields, so a store answering whole sessions leaks neither entries nor credentials](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L201)).
+- A row's times go on the wire as ISO strings whatever the store answered with, a `Date`, a string or an epoch number, so a store written against the shape the wire shows is not a failed read ([validated by: puts a time on the wire as an ISO string whatever shape the store answered with](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L148)).
+- The list is the connecting user's, taken from the connection: a frame carrying a `userId` of somebody else is answered with the caller's own conversations, and the store is asked for the caller's id ([validated by: answers the connecting user's own conversations even when the frame names another user](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L125)).
+- `limit` and `before` reach the store as the frame sent them, so a client pages the socket the way a consumer pages the store ([validated by: carries a limit and a cursor through to the store as the frame sent them](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L136)).
+- The conversation the connection is itself in is listed like any other, so a fresh connect sees a row of its own holding no entries ([validated by: lists the conversation the connection is in, like any other, holding no entries yet](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L261)).
+- A client that names a `?sessionId=` the server will ignore, because resume is off, is reported rather than passed over in silence, since the client is handed a new session with nothing said ([validated by: warns when a client names a session id that resume being off makes the server ignore](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L183)).
+- It is reported once per engine, not once per upgrade, since the line is a deployment fault and a per-upgrade one could be flooded by any client; a second engine in the same process still reports its own ([validated by: warns once per engine, so a second engine in the same process reports its own ignored id](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L225)).
+- A list is worth nothing to a client unless `transport.resume` is on too, since `?sessionId=` is read only under resume: the engine warns at startup for history without it, and the docs say so where they tell a client to reconnect with a row's id ([validated by: warns at startup when history is on without resume, since no row could then be opened](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L172)).
+- A client picks a conversation by reconnecting with `?sessionId=` from a row, which session resume already authorises and replays: there is no switch frame and no second replay path ([validated by: hands a client an id it can rejoin the conversation with](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L474)).
+- One read is in flight per socket: ten frames arriving in a tick are ten answers and one query at a time, since nothing else in the transport stops a client turning frames into parallel database reads ([validated by: reads the store once at a time per socket, however many frames arrive in one tick](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L399)).
+- A `list_conversations` sent while a turn is streaming is answered in the middle of it — after the turn's user entry and before the assistant entry it is still producing — because a history request is not a turn and waits on no lock ([validated by: answers a list in the middle of a streaming turn, before that turn ends](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L426)).
+
+## When the frame cannot be answered
+
+- Without `transport.history`, the frame is answered with an `error` of code `UNSUPPORTED` and no list ([validated by: refuses the frame when conversation history is not turned on](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L238)).
+- A store implementing no `listFor` is answered with the same code, never an empty list: a client cannot tell "no conversations" from "the server could not look", and a history view is the one place that difference matters ([validated by: refuses the frame when the store cannot list, rather than reporting no conversations](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L289)).
+- A handler built without a store answers the frame too, with the same `UNSUPPORTED`: a client left waiting on a frame nothing will answer is the one outcome the engine never produces ([validated by: answers a handler built with no store at all, rather than leaving the client waiting](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L276)).
+- A store that throws `StoreCannotList` is answered `UNSUPPORTED` rather than `SERVER_ERROR`, and logged by that name, so a collection injected without a `find` reads as the misconfiguration it is rather than as a database that failed ([validated by: tells a store that cannot list apart from one that failed, so a misconfiguration is not a blip](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L301)).
+- Only the read and the mapping of what it answered are attributed to the store: a send that throws is reported as handling the message, not as the store having failed, and the next list on that socket is still answered ([validated by: blames a send that threw on the socket, not the store, and still answers the next list](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L370)).
+- A store that fails is answered `SERVER_ERROR` with none of the store's own words, the socket stays open, and the log carries the session id, the user id and the error's type rather than its message, since a driver's message can hold a connection string and its password ([validated by: answers a failing store with a server error, keeps the socket open, and logs no word the store said](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L324)).
+
+- A store that rejects with something that is not an `Error` is logged by that value's type, so a driver throwing a string is still described ([validated by: reports the type of a rejection that is not an Error, rather than failing to describe it](../../src/transport/conversationList.test.ts#L354)).
+
+## Reading the frame
+
+- A `list_conversations` naming no window is accepted, and a field the frame does not define — a `userId`, for one — is dropped rather than forwarded ([validated by: accepts list_conversations with no window, and keeps no field the frame does not define](../../src/transport/ws/validation.test.ts#L23)).
+- `limit` is accepted as a whole number from 1 to 200, the bound the store caps a page at ([validated by: accepts a whole-number limit inside the page bound and a cursor carrying both halves](../../src/transport/ws/validation.test.ts#L29)).
+- Anything else is refused rather than clamped, with an error naming `limit`, since a limit of `0`, `201`, `1.5`, `'10'` or `NaN` is a client bug worth reporting ([validated by: refuses a limit that is not a whole number from one to two hundred, naming the field](../../src/transport/ws/validation.test.ts#L37)).
+- `before` is accepted only carrying both halves, an ISO `updatedAt` and a `sessionId` of the shape the upgrade reads one in, and is refused with an error naming `before`: a cursor missing its id would page on a time alone and skip a conversation on a tie ([validated by: refuses a cursor missing either half, or carrying a time nothing can parse, naming the field](../../src/transport/ws/validation.test.ts#L48)).
+- The validator dispatches through a `Map` rather than an object literal, so a frame whose `type` names an inherited member — `constructor`, `__proto__`, `toString` — reads as an unknown type; the `switch` the table replaced was equally safe, an object keyed by the frame's own `type` would not have been ([validated by: answers a frame naming an inherited member as an unknown type, rather than reaching it](../../src/transport/ws/validation.test.ts#L66)).
